Flags:
      --set=<name>=<value> ..., --set-file=<name>=<filename> ...
          Key-value pairs to set on the instance.
      --visual
          Open an editor to modify fields visually.
      --cmd
          Run a command to edit fields (receives YAML on stdin, outputs edited YAML on stdout).
      --load, --save=<FILE>
          Load fields from a YAML file.
      --dry-run
          Print patches without applying them.
  -f, --field
          Specify which fields to include in the output.
  -o, --output
          Output format. One of: kv, table, json, yaml, raw, quiet, template.
      --follow
          Follow instance logs after creation.

Global flags:
  -h, --help
          Show context-sensitive help.
      --config=<file> ($UNIKRAFT_CONFIG)
          Path to the configuration file.
      --log-level=<level> ($UNIKRAFT_LOG_LEVEL)
          Set the logging level.
          [default: info, choices: trace, debug, info, warn, error, fatal]
      --log-type=<type> ($UNIKRAFT_LOG_TYPE)
          Set the log type.
          [default: text, choices: text, json]
      --profile=<name> ($UNIKRAFT_PROFILE)
          Set the current profile.
      --[no-]telemetry ($UNIKRAFT_TELEMETRY)
          Toggle usage analytics.
          [default: true]
      --timeout=<duration> ($UNIKRAFT_TIMEOUT)
          Set a deadline for the command (e.g. 30s, 5m, 1h).

Create flags:
      --metro=<metro>
          Metro to deploy in. Defaults to the profile's default metro.
          [examples: fra, sfo]
  -n, --name=<name>
          Instance name: a lowercase DNS label of up to 118 characters, unique per metro. Generated from the image name when omitted.
      --tag=<tag> ...
          Tags for grouping and filtering: up to 16, each 1 to 256 characters of letters, digits and -+_.:=. Not visible to the guest.
          [example: env-prod]
      --annotation=<key>=<value> ...
          key=value metadata, up to 256 entries; keys are Kubernetes-style [prefix/]name. Unlike tags, annotations reach the guest in its start data.
          [examples: env=production, example.com/team=platform]
      --image=<name>:<tag>
          Image to deploy, as an OCI reference such as nginx:latest or a full registry URL. Required unless template, branch or checkpoint is given, and cannot be combined with them.
          [examples: nginx:latest, my-app:v1.2.3]
      --pull-policy=<policy>
          When to pull the image relative to the node's cache: always, if_not_present or never.
          [examples: always, if_not_present, never]
      --type=<type>
          Virtual machine type: micro (Firecracker microVM, the default) or full (QEMU; required for GPUs, no scale-to-zero, templates, branching or checkpoints).
          [examples: micro, full]
      --args=<arg>
          Command-line arguments passed to the instance at start, up to 128.
          [example: --port=8080]
  -e, --env=<key>=<value> ...
          Environment variable as KEY=value, up to 256.
          [example: DEBUG=true]
  -m, --memory=<size>
          Memory for the instance, such as 128MiB or 1GiB. Defaults to 128MiB; the plan sets the allowed range (see metro quotas limits.memory).
          [examples: 128MiB, 1GiB]
      --vcpus=<n>
          Number of vCPUs, default 1; the plan sets the allowed range (see metro quotas limits.vcpus).
          [examples: 1, 2, 4]
      --gpus=<n>
          Number of GPUs to attach, 0 or 1. Requires type full and a plan with GPU support; the GPU stays assigned until the instance is deleted. Not with template, branch or checkpoint.
          [examples: 0, 1]
      --service=<name>
          Service group to join, by name or UUID. Leave unset and give service.services or service.domains to create a group owned by the instance; without any service the instance is reachable only privately.
  -p, --publish=<src>:<dest>[/<handlers>] ...
          Published port as PORT:DESTINATION/HANDLERS. Handlers: http (per-request load balancing), tls (TLS terminated by the platform), redirect. Port 80 must be http, 443 must be http+tls, other TCP ports tls only; 80:443/http+redirect redirects to HTTPS.
          [examples: 443:8080/http+tls, 80:443/http+redirect]
      --domain=<fqdn> ...
          Domain for the group: a bare label such as myapp becomes a subdomain of the metro, a dotted name such as example.com is a custom domain whose certificate is issued automatically unless given as name=example.com,certificate=my-cert. Up to 8.
          [examples: example.com, myapp]
  -v, --volume=<name>:<path>[:<options>] ...
          Volume to attach as NAME:/MOUNT[:ro]; the mount path must be absolute. Up to 4 per instance.
          [examples: my-vol:/data, cache:/tmp:ro]
      --rom=name=<name>,image=<ref>,at=<path> ...
          Read-only ROM device from an image or a local directory; the name uses letters, digits, - and _ and appears as /dev/ukp_rom_NAME in the guest. Up to 8.
          [examples: name=my-rom,image=myuser/my-rom:latest,at=/rom0, name=mydata,dir=./mydata,at=/rom]
      --plugin=name=<name>,image=<ref>[,config=<json>] ...
          Plugin loaded from an image, with an optional JSON config passed to it on start. Up to 8 per instance.
          [examples: name=sandbox,image=plugins/sandbox:latest, name=sandbox,image=plugins/sandbox:latest,config={"persist_path":"/data"}]
      --scale-to-zero=<key>=<value>
          Scale-to-zero options; requires a service group.
            policy: on | idle (also while open TCP connections are idle; needs stateful=true) | off
            cooldown-time: ms of inactivity before scaling to zero (default 1000, minimum 100)
            notify-time: ms the instance is warned beforehand; 0 disables, must be below cooldown-time
            stateful: true keeps RAM in a snapshot and resumes from it, false restarts from scratch.
          [examples: on, policy=on,cooldown-time=300, policy=on,stateful=true,cooldown-time=500,notify-time=100]
      --autokill=<key>=<value>
          Autokill options.
            time: time after the instance stops before it is deleted, such as 5s or 24h; 0 disables
            num-requests: delete the instance after it has served this many requests or connections.
          [examples: time=5s, num-requests=100, time=5s,num-requests=100]
      --restart=<policy>
          Restart policy: never (default), always (restart whenever the instance exits by itself) or on-failure (only after a crash). Restarts back off from immediate up to 5m. Not with the delete-on-stop feature.
          [examples: always, on-failure, never]
      --sched-priority=<priority>
          Scheduling priority: normal (default), medium, high or admin. Requires the override_vm_priority permission.
          [examples: normal, medium, high, admin]
      --autostart
          Start the instance right after creation. When false it stays stopped, except that scale-to-zero puts it in standby, where incoming traffic starts it.
      --replicas=<n>
          Additional identical instances to create; replicas=2 creates three in total.
          [examples: 1, 3]
      --feature=<feature> ...
          Instance feature: delete-on-stop deletes the instance when it stops (not with a restart policy or delete-lock); nested-virt enables nested virtualisation and needs a special permission.
          [example: delete-on-stop]
      --template=<name>
          Create from an instance template, by name or UUID; the clone inherits its configuration and, when the template has a snapshot, its memory state. Not with image, branch, checkpoint, type or gpus.
      --branch=<instance>
          Clone an existing instance by name or UUID; a running source is snapshotted asynchronously and keeps running. Not with image, args, env, memory, vcpus, type or gpus.
      --checkpoint=<checkpoint>
          Create from a checkpoint by name or UUID, restoring its memory and disk. Same restrictions as branch.
      --rm
          Automatically delete the instance when it stops.
